{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkWyXQ3btH4kBYLc23sK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UdMSTvoZaqushAqNNybXA7","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with a G clef, one flat, and no time signature. Bar 85: a dotted C5 8th and a D5 16th, then the beamed C5-D5-C5-D5 figure in 32nds under a slur. After the barline come three identical bars. Each has C5 8th beamed to A4 16th, then a rest, then Bb4 8th beamed to G4 16th, then a rest. These are bars 86, 87 and 88. Next barline, bar 89: a 16th group A4-G4-F4 with a small numeral under it (the edition's triplet). Then E4-F4-G4, then an F4 quarter with a tie running over the barline. The last stretch is bar 90. It has F4-G4-F#4 as 16ths, then G4-Bb4 at the right edge. The line breaks partway through bar 90, so the next line finishes it. Pitches and rhythm match the edition throughout. The only differences are notation: the copyist beams 8th + 16th pairs, and the triplet numeral is barely legible.","right":true,"insights":"This line was easy to match. Bar 85's dotted rhythm and 32nd figure is distinctive, and so is the switch to triplet 16ths in bar 89. They anchor both sides of the three identical bars 86–88. The hardest part was the right edge: the line stops partway through bar 90, so the end has to be read from a few cramped notes. A little more of the image on the right, or the start of the next line, would have confirmed it."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5514,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5514},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"04fece70-a77e-4